Z||000000||sqoach||11-29-2000||12:12 AM||||me and a friend are painting some interior parts on his 96 civic.  i was wondering what is the best way to go about this?  and if any1 has ever done this with a civic or knows some1 that has, could you tell me how to get some of the trim pieces off, or what book to get that would show it?  thanks.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||11-29-2000||12:27 AM||||please not bright yellow...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000002||sqoach||11-29-2000||12:40 AM||||ewwww...yellow would look $hi++y.  we're going with a blue color that is really close to the exterior color.  i'm just wondering if we should sand the trim pieces or use that paint sticking stuff.  he kind of wants to keep the texture on the trim, that's why the undecidedness is there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RNGCRSH||11-29-2000||12:59 AM||||There is actually a special paint just for interior pieces. its made to stick to vynil. you just have to make sure that all dirt and grease is removed. no sanding involved. the paint is more like a dye, the texture will still show through. you can get it at just about any auto paint supplier. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||11-29-2000||01:00 AM||||Clean them, clean them and then clean them again. Before you paint, clean them!!!!! It is next to impossible to get all the Armor All/silicone off the interior pieces. Marhyde makes interior plastic/vinyl color spray. It can also be custom mixed by an auto paint supplier. I personally like the custom mixed stuff because you can get it in different degrees of gloss- not to dull/not to glossy. Just get it mixed a little glossy and have them give you some "flattner" to mix with it so you can get the look you want.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RNGCRSH||11-29-2000||01:10 AM||||Well said Cooter. I forgot to mention how aggresivly you must clean. use a soft bristle brush,dish soap and loads of elbow grease. then prep with paint manufacturers recomendations.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||sqoach||11-29-2000||01:13 AM||||cool.  thanks guys.  we were thinking about just doing plastic parts, but since there's a paint for the vinyl, we might try that.  and we should make sure the paint is made specially for interiors? the main problem now is getting all those stupid trim pieces out of the dash. <P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||10
Z||000007||william_thorniii||11-29-2000||08:08 AM||||sqoach,<P>The "plastic" is vinyl.  Use the vinyl paint on any plastic or plastic derivate material inside of the vehicle.  I re-painted my 85 GT interior.  It looks much better than before. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
